# Words 151-200
R = [
("VRB-151","thank – thanked – thanked – thanking","'Thank' takes the person directly, with no 'to'.","THANK and THINK: when you think of someone's help, you thank them.","'Thank you so much' / 'Thanks a lot' — 'Thanks' is the casual form.","Write a sentence thanking a teacher for something specific.",("someone helps you","feel grateful","say 'Thank you for your help.'"),"thank vs thanks","Thank is the verb (I thank you). Thanks is the noun or casual form (Thanks! / Many thanks)."),
("VRB-152","congratulate – congratulated – congratulated – congratulating","Use 'congratulate someone ON something', not 'to' or 'for'.","CONGRATULATE has a T in the middle, not a D: congraTulate.","'Congratulations!' (with -s) is what you say directly.","Write a sentence congratulating a friend on their new job.",("hear good news","go to the person","say 'Congratulations on your success!'"),"congratulate vs praise","Congratulate = say you're happy about someone's success or event. Praise = say someone did something well."),
("VRB-153","forgive – forgave – forgiven – forgiving","'Forgive' takes the person directly, with no 'to'.","FOR-GIVE: give up your anger FOR someone.","'Forgive me' is a strong apology; 'Excuse me' is for small things.","Write a sentence about someone you forgave.",("someone hurts you","they apologise","let go of the anger"),"forgive vs forget","Forgive = stop being angry. Forget = stop remembering. You can forgive without forgetting."),
("VRB-154","trust – trusted – trusted – trusting","'Trust' usually takes a person directly. 'Trust in' is for big ideas like God or fate.","TRUST: you can Rely Upon Someone Totally.","'Trust me' is a common promise. A 'trust' is also a charity organisation.","Write a sentence about a person you trust completely.",("get to know someone","see them keep promises","feel you can rely on them"),"trust vs believe","Believe = think something is true. Trust = be sure someone is honest and reliable over time."),
("VRB-155","love – loved – loved – loving","'Love' is a state verb, so it's normally used in the simple form. 'I'm loving it' is informal advertising style.","LOVE: the heart shape ♥ looks like a V inside the word.","'I'd love to' is a warm way to accept an invitation.","Write a sentence about a hobby you love.",("meet someone","spend time together","feel strong affection"),"love vs like","Like = find pleasant. Love = feel strong affection. 'I like tea; I love my family.'"),
("VRB-156","like – liked – liked – liking","'Like' is a state verb, so it normally doesn't take -ing.","LIKE on social media: one small tap = you find it pleasant.","'Would you like…?' is a polite offer. 'Do you like…?' asks about taste.","Write a sentence about a food you like and one you don't.",("try something","enjoy it","want it again"),"like vs would like","Do you like tea? = in general. Would you like tea? = right now, an offer."),
("VRB-157","hate – hated – hated – hating","'Hate' is a state verb, so it normally doesn't take -ing.","HATE is LOVE's opposite; both have 4 letters.","'I hate to say this, but…' softens bad news.","Write a sentence about a chore you hate.",("meet something unpleasant","feel strong dislike","want to avoid it"),"hate vs dislike","Dislike = not like (mild). Hate = dislike very strongly."),
("VRB-158","respect – respected – respected – respecting","'Respect' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","RE-SPECT: 're' = back, 'spect' = look: look up to someone.","'With respect, …' is a polite way to disagree.","Write a sentence about a person you respect and why.",("see someone's good actions","admire their qualities","treat them with care"),"respect vs obey","Respect = admire and value. Obey = follow orders. You can respect someone without obeying them."),
("VRB-159","support – supported – supported – supporting","'Support' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","SUP-PORT: a port supports ships; you support people.","'Customer support' is the help desk. 'Which team do you support?' = which do you cheer for.","Write a sentence about a person who supports you.",("a friend has a hard time","stay with them and help","they feel stronger"),"support vs help","Help = make a task easier. Support = help and encourage over time, often emotionally."),
("VRB-160","help – helped – helped – helping","Use 'help someone WITH something', not 'for'.","HELP: four letters, the first word to shout in trouble.","'Can I help you?' is what shop staff say. 'Help yourself' = take what you want.","Write a sentence about how you help at home.",("someone struggles","do part of the work","the task becomes easy"),"help vs assist","Same meaning. Assist is more formal (a hospital, an office)."),
("VRB-161","share – shared – shared – sharing","The thing comes right after 'share', then 'with + person'.","SHARE: split one thing so each person has a part.","'Share' online = post or forward: 'Share this video.'","Write a sentence about something you shared with a friend.",("have one thing","give part to a friend","both enjoy it"),"share vs divide","Divide = split into parts. Share = divide AND give parts to others, or use together."),
("VRB-162","care – cared – cared – caring","'Care for' = look after. 'Care about' = feel it matters. 'Take care of' also means look after.","CARE sounds like CAR: you take care of a new car.","'Take care!' is a friendly goodbye.","Write a sentence about someone you take care of.",("someone is ill","bring food and medicine","they get better"),"care for vs care about","Care FOR = look after (care for a patient). Care ABOUT = feel something is important (care about the environment)."),
("VRB-163","comfort – comforted – comforted – comforting","'Comfort' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","COMFORT has 'FORT': a strong place where you feel safe.","'Comfort food' is food that makes you feel good.","Write a sentence about how you comfort a sad friend.",("a friend is crying","sit with them and speak kindly","they feel a little better"),"comfort vs console","Same idea. Console is more formal and used for serious loss."),
("VRB-164","encourage – encouraged – encouraged – encouraging","Use 'encourage someone TO + verb', not 'for'.","EN-COURAGE: put COURAGE into someone.","'Keep going!' and 'You can do it!' are encouraging lines.","Write a sentence about someone who encouraged you to try something new.",("someone feels unsure","tell them they can do it","they try confidently"),"encourage vs force","Encourage = give confidence so someone chooses to act. Force = make them act without choice."),
("VRB-165","praise – praised – praised – praising","'Praise' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","PRAISE rhymes with RAISE: praise raises someone's confidence.","'Praise someone FOR something': 'She praised him for his honesty.'","Write a sentence about a time a teacher praised you.",("someone does good work","notice it","say it was excellent"),"praise vs prize","Praise = kind words. Prize = an award you win."),
("VRB-166","blame – blamed – blamed – blaming","Use 'blame someone FOR something' or 'blame something ON someone'. Not both together.","BLAME: point a finger — the B looks like a pointing hand.","'I don't blame you' means your reaction is understandable.","Write a sentence about something people often blame on the weather.",("something goes wrong","look for who caused it","say it's their fault"),"blame for vs blame on","Blame the driver FOR the accident. Blame the accident ON the driver. Same meaning, different order."),
("VRB-167","criticize – criticized – criticized – criticizing","'Criticize' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","CRITIC-IZE: act like a film CRITIC.","'Constructive criticism' means comments meant to help you improve.","Write a sentence about how to criticize someone politely.",("see a mistake","point out what's wrong","suggest a fix"),"criticize vs complain","Criticize = point out faults in someone or their work. Complain = say you're unhappy about something."),
("VRB-168","tease – teased – teased – teasing","Use 'tease someone ABOUT something', not 'of'.","TEASE sounds like 'tees-ing': friends tee you up for a joke.","Teasing is usually friendly; if it hurts, it becomes bullying.","Write a sentence about how your siblings or friends tease you.",("notice a funny habit","joke about it playfully","everyone laughs"),"tease vs bully","Tease = joke in a light, friendly way. Bully = hurt or scare someone again and again."),
("VRB-169","reconcile – reconciled – reconciled – reconciling","'Reconcile' already means come together again, so 'together' is not needed.","RE-CONCILE: 're' = again, 'concile' = come together (like council).","Banks: 'reconcile accounts' means make two records match.","Write a sentence about two people who reconciled after a fight.",("two friends fight","they talk and apologise","they are friends again"),"reconcile vs compromise","Reconcile = become friends again. Compromise = each side gives up something to agree."),
("VRB-170","marry – married – married – marrying","'Marry' takes the person directly, with no 'with'. 'Get married TO someone' is also correct.","MARRY → MARRIED: Y becomes I before -ed.","'Get married' is more common in speech: 'They got married in May.'","Write a sentence about when someone in your family got married.",("two people decide to marry","the wedding ceremony happens","they are husband and wife"),"marry vs get married","She married him. / She got married to him. / They got married. Never 'married with'."),
("VRB-171","divorce – divorced – divorced – divorcing","'Divorce' takes the person directly. 'Get divorced from' is also correct.","DI-VORCE: 'di' = apart, two ways.","'Divorced' is also an adjective: 'He is divorced.'","Write a neutral sentence explaining what 'divorce' means.",("a marriage has problems","go to court","the marriage legally ends"),"divorce vs separate","Separate = live apart, still married. Divorce = legally end the marriage."),
("VRB-172","date – dated – dated – dating","As 'go out romantically', 'date' takes the person directly, with no 'with'.","DATE a person = fix DATES to meet.","'Date' also means a calendar day or a fruit (khajoor).","Write a sentence explaining 'date' as a calendar word.",("like someone","go out together a few times","become a couple"),"date vs meet","Meet = see someone, any reason. Date = meet someone romantically."),
("VRB-173","flirt – flirted – flirted – flirting","'Flirt' always needs 'with' before the person.","FLIRT: F for Fun, light and playful.","'Flirting' at work can make people uncomfortable; know the setting.","Write a sentence explaining the difference between being friendly and flirting.",("notice someone you like","smile and joke playfully","see if they respond"),"flirt vs tease","Tease = joke about someone, friendly. Flirt = behave playfully to show romantic interest."),
("VRB-174","hug – hugged – hugged – hugging","'Hug' takes the person directly, with no 'with'.","HUG → HUGGED: double G, like two arms around someone.","'Give someone a hug' is very common.","Write a sentence about who you hug when you come home.",("see a loved one","put your arms around them","feel warm and happy"),"hug vs embrace","Same meaning. Embrace is more formal and can also mean accept an idea: 'embrace change'."),
("VRB-175","kiss – kissed – kissed – kissing","'Kiss' takes the person directly, with no 'to'.","KISS has double S, like two lips.","In many cultures, a kiss on the cheek is a greeting.","Write a sentence about how people greet each other in another country you know.",("lean close","touch with your lips","step back and smile"),"kiss vs hug","Hug = hold with arms. Kiss = touch with lips."),
("VRB-176","betray – betrayed – betrayed – betraying","'Betray' takes a direct object; don't add 'on'.","BE-TRAY: serve someone's secret on a TRAY to others.","'Betray' is strong: it's about broken trust.","Write a sentence about a character in a film who betrayed a friend.",("someone trusts you","you break that trust","they feel hurt"),"betray vs cheat","Cheat = act dishonestly to gain something. Betray = break someone's trust, especially a friend's or country's."),
("VRB-177","gossip – gossiped – gossiped – gossiping","Use 'gossip ABOUT someone', not 'of'.","GOSSIP has double S, like two people whispering 'sss'.","'Office gossip' is talk about colleagues' private lives.","Write a sentence about why gossip can be harmful.",("hear a rumour","talk about it with others","the story spreads"),"gossip vs discuss","Discuss = talk seriously about a topic. Gossip = talk about other people's private lives, often unkindly."),
("VRB-178","socialize – socialized – socialized – socializing","Use 'socialize WITH people', not 'to'.","SOCIAL-IZE: be SOCIAL.","British spelling: socialise.","Write a sentence about where you like to socialize.",("go to an event","talk and laugh with people","make new friends"),"socialize vs meet","Meet = come together with someone. Socialize = spend relaxed, friendly time with people."),
("VRB-179","bond – bonded – bonded – bonding","'Bond with' already means come together, so 'together' is not needed.","BOND like JAMES BOND: a strong link.","'Team bonding' activities help colleagues get close.","Write a sentence about how you bonded with a new friend.",("meet someone new","share experiences","feel close"),"bond vs connect","Connect = link or relate. Bond = form a deep, lasting emotional connection."),
("VRB-180","connect – connected – connected – connecting","Use 'connect TO' for devices and networks, 'connect WITH' for people and feelings.","CON-NECT: like a NECKlace that links beads.","'Connecting flight' = a second flight you change to.","Write a sentence about how you connect to the internet at home.",("see two wires","join them","power flows"),"connect to vs connect with","Connect TO a network or device. Connect WITH a person or an idea."),
("VRB-181","become – became – become – becoming","'Become' is followed directly by a noun or adjective, never 'to be'.","BE + COME: you COME to BE something.","Hindi speakers often say 'became to be'; English needs only 'became'.","Write a sentence about what you want to become in five years.",("be a student","study and work hard","become an engineer"),"become vs get","Both mean change state. Get is informal (get angry). Become is neutral or formal (become famous)."),
("VRB-182","change – changed – changed – changing","To exchange something, use 'change it FOR something else', not 'with'.","CHANGE: C for Coins — you get change after paying.","'Change' as a noun also means coins: 'Keep the change.'","Write a sentence about something you would like to change in your routine.",("things are one way","something happens","they are different"),"change vs exchange","Change = make different. Exchange = give one thing and get another. 'Change clothes'; 'exchange a gift'.","I will change this shirt with a bigger size.","I will change this shirt for a bigger size."),
("VRB-183","grow – grew – grown – growing","After 'has', use the past participle 'grown'.","GROW → GREW → GROWN, like THROW → THREW → THROWN.","'Grow up' = become an adult. 'What do you want to be when you grow up?'","Write a sentence about a plant or vegetable you have grown.",("plant a seed","water it daily","it becomes a plant"),"grow vs grow up","Grow = get bigger (plants, numbers, people). Grow up = become an adult (only people)."),
("VRB-184","develop – developed – developed – developing","For something continuing up to now, use the present perfect with 'for': 'have wanted… for a long time'.","DEVELOP: a photo develops slowly from nothing to a clear picture.","'Developing country' and 'app developer' are common phrases.","Write a sentence about a skill you want to develop this year.",("start with a basic skill","practise and improve","become advanced"),"develop vs improve","Improve = make better. Develop = grow over time, or build something new (develop an app)."),
("VRB-185","increase – increased – increased – increasing","Use 'by' for the amount of change and 'to' for the final number.","INCREASE: IN + CREASE: more folds, more size.","Verb: in-CREASE. Noun: IN-crease ('a big increase').","Write a sentence about a price that increased recently.",("the price is ₹100","it goes up","it becomes ₹110"),"increase by vs increase to","Increased BY 10% = the change. Increased TO ₹110 = the new total."),
("VRB-186","decrease – decreased – decreased – decreasing","Use 'by' for the amount of change and 'to' for the final number.","DE-CREASE: 'de' = down.","Verb: de-CREASE. Noun: DE-crease.","Write a sentence about something that decreased in your city.",("the price is ₹100","it goes down","it becomes ₹80"),"decrease vs reduce","Decrease can happen by itself (sales decreased). Reduce is usually done by someone (we reduced costs)."),
("VRB-187","improve – improved – improved – improving","'Improve' takes a direct object, with no 'of'.","IM-PROVE: PROVE yourself a little better each day.","'Room for improvement' = it's OK, but could be better.","Write a sentence about how you improved your English this year.",("have a weak skill","practise regularly","the skill gets better"),"improve vs increase","Improve = make better in quality. Increase = make bigger in number or amount."),
("VRB-188","expand – expanded – expanded – expanding","Use 'expand INTO' for new markets or areas.","EX-PAND: PAN out, spread wider.","'Expand on' = give more detail: 'Can you expand on that point?'","Write a sentence about a business that expanded to new cities.",("a shop is small","it opens more branches","it covers a bigger area"),"expand vs extend","Expand = get bigger in all directions. Extend = get longer (in time or one direction)."),
("VRB-189","shrink – shrank – shrunk – shrinking","'Shrink' is irregular: shrink – shrank – shrunk.","SHRINK → SHRANK → SHRUNK, like DRINK → DRANK → DRUNK.","'Shrink' is also informal for a psychiatrist.","Write a sentence about clothes that shrank in the wash.",("wash a cotton shirt in hot water","it gets smaller","it no longer fits"),"shrink vs reduce","Shrink = get smaller by itself, often in size. Reduce = make smaller on purpose."),
("VRB-190","transform – transformed – transformed – transforming","Use 'transform into', not 'transform to'.","TRANS-FORM: change across to a new FORM, like Transformers.","'Digital transformation' means a company moving its work online.","Write a sentence about a place near you that has transformed.",("see an old building","renovate it completely","it becomes a modern café"),"transform vs change","Change can be small. Transform means a complete, dramatic change."),
("VRB-191","convert – converted – converted – converting","Use 'convert into' or 'convert to', not 'in'.","CON-VERT: turn around ('vert' = turn), like converting a file.","'Convert ₹ to $' in currency; 'convert Word to PDF' on a computer.","Write a sentence about a file or unit you have converted.",("have a Word file","use a converter","get a PDF"),"convert vs change","Convert = change from one form or system to another. Change is the general word."),
("VRB-192","adapt – adapted – adapted – adapting","When it means 'adjust yourself', use 'adapt TO something'.","ADAPT: an ADAPTER lets a plug fit a new socket.","'Adapt' also means change a book into a film: 'adapted from a novel'.","Write a sentence about how you adapted to a new city or school.",("move to a new place","change your habits","feel at home"),"adapt vs adopt","Adapt = change to fit. Adopt = take a child or an idea as your own."),
("VRB-193","adjust – adjusted – adjusted – adjusting","Use 'adjust TO' when it means get used to something.","AD-JUST: make it JUST right.","'Adjust' is very common in Indian English meaning 'manage with less'. Standard English prefers 'manage' or 'make do'.","Write a sentence about how you adjusted to a new timetable.",("the chair is too low","move the lever","it's at the right height"),"adjust vs adapt","Adjust = make small changes. Adapt = make bigger changes to fit a new situation."),
("VRB-194","evolve – evolved – evolved – evolving","Use 'evolve INTO' for the new form something becomes.","E-VOLVE: slowly roll out ('volve' = roll) over time.","'Evolution' is the noun, used in science for slow change over generations.","Write a sentence about how mobile phones have evolved.",("a simple form exists","it changes slowly over years","a new, advanced form appears"),"evolve vs develop","Develop = grow or improve (can be fast). Evolve = change gradually over a long time.","Mobile phones have evolved to smartphones.","Mobile phones have evolved into smartphones."),
("VRB-195","appear – appeared – appeared – appearing","When it means 'seem', 'appear' is a state verb and isn't used in the -ing form.","AP-PEAR: something PEARs up — becomes visible.","'Appear' also means come before a court or on TV: 'appear for an exam' is Indian English for 'take an exam'.","Write a sentence using 'appears' to describe how someone looks today.",("nothing is visible","something comes into view","you can see it"),"appear vs seem","Seem = give an impression. Appear = the same, slightly more formal, and it also means become visible."),
("VRB-196","disappear – disappeared – disappeared – disappearing","'Disappear' can't take a direct object. The thing itself disappears.","DIS + APPEAR = the opposite of appear. One S, two Ps.","'Disappear' is often used for people who go missing.","Write a sentence about something that disappeared from your room.",("the object is on the table","you look away","it's gone"),"disappear vs vanish","Same meaning. Vanish sounds more sudden and dramatic."),
("VRB-197","occur – occurred – occurred – occurring","An idea occurs TO a person, not in the mind.","OCCUR → OCCURRED: double R before -ed.","'It never occurred to me' = I never thought of it.","Write a sentence about an idea that occurred to you recently.",("you're thinking of something else","an idea arrives","you remember it"),"occur vs happen","Same meaning. Occur is more formal. Only 'occur' is used for ideas: 'It occurred to me that…'.","An idea occurred in my mind.","An idea occurred to me."),
("VRB-198","happen – happened – happened – happening","After 'is', use the -ing form: 'is happening'.","HAPPEN has double P, like two events in a row.","'What happened?' is the most common way to ask about a problem.","Write a sentence about something funny that happened this week.",("everything is normal","an event takes place","people talk about it"),"happen vs occur","Same meaning. Happen is everyday. Occur is more formal."),
("VRB-199","exist – existed – existed – existing","'Exist' is a state verb, so it isn't normally used in the -ing form.","EXIST: something that EXISTS can be pointed at, like an EXIT sign.","'Existing customers' = customers you already have.","Write a sentence about an animal that no longer exists.",("something comes into being","it is present in the world","people can see it"),"exist vs live","Live = be alive, or have your home somewhere. Exist = be real or present at all."),
("VRB-200","remain – remained – remained – remaining","After 'remain' (like 'be'), use an adjective (silent), not a noun (silence).","RE-MAIN: stay in the MAIN place.","'Remaining' = left over: 'the remaining money'.","Write a sentence about how you remain calm under pressure.",("everyone leaves","you stay","you are still there"),"remain vs stay","Same meaning. Remain is more formal: 'Please remain seated.'"),
]
